00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:24.160
But anyway, to understand how Microsoft came
to dominate this world, we first have to understand

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.569
how code used to be stored and distributed
before the 2000s because it was really bad.

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:31.819
I mean just think about it.

00:03:31.819 --> 00:03:36.129
When you have thousands of coders who are
working on the same codebase and making millions

00:03:36.129 --> 00:03:41.470
of edits to add new features and fix bugs,
you’re naturally gonna run into a lot of

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:42.470
issues.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:45.790
Joe’s bug revisions are gonna end up breaking
Bob’s new feature.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:49.569
John’s new efficient algorithm might’ve
forgotten a critical edge case for Mary’s

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:51.830
legacy functionality and so on and so forth.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:56.920
The bottomline is that getting code from thousands
of engineers to work together in unison is

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:01.950
a very difficult task in itself, and for the
longest time, the solution for this issue

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:03.740
was simply brute force.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:08.689
Every tech company would have their own databases,
servers, and revision control systems, and

00:04:08.689 --> 00:04:13.530
dozens of code librarians and senior engineers
would be hired to simply maintain a healthy

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:14.530
codebase.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:18.100
These guys wouldn’t write their own code
or algorithms or any of that.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:23.100
Instead, their full time job was to review
each and every change to ensure that none

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:27.680
of them would break any existing feature or
conflict with parallel changes.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.010
Honestly, this seems like a pretty tedious
and annoying job but it was also very much

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:38.970
critical and necessary until a young man named
Linus Torvalds decided to step in.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:42.919
Just kidding, Linus didn’t care about this
internal software engineering issue.

00:04:42.919 --> 00:04:46.890
He was looking to fry much bigger fish and
compete directly with Microsoft.

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:51.110
If you haven’t figured it out yet, Linus
was the creator of Linux and that’s where

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:54.050
he spent the first 15 years of his professional
life.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:58.670
But eventually after Linux grew to be much
bigger than just himself, he would turn his

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:04.050
attention to one his biggest frustrations
of software engineering: version control.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:08.970
Linus was aware of this issue more than anyone
else as he was the head of the biggest open

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:10.600
source project of all time.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:15.450
At least with companies, you can be sure that
every engineer is skilled and has the companies

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:20.449
best interest in mind atleast to some degree
because well they’re getting paid.

00:05:20.449 --> 00:05:25.169
With open source software on the other hand,
literally anyone can contribute so it was

00:05:25.169 --> 00:05:29.259
up to people like Linus to ensure that only
the positive changes made their way onto the

00:05:29.259 --> 00:05:30.300
repo.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:35.639
This was always a pain in the butt for Linus
but he put up with it as he was more focused

00:05:35.639 --> 00:05:37.270
on the bigger picture.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:42.800
But, on one fateful night in 2005 when working
on the Linux Kernel, he would reach his wits

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.100
end with crappy version control.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:49.699
He vowed right then and there that he was
gonna solve this issue once and for all.

00:05:49.699 --> 00:05:54.509
And with that, he would go offline for 2 weeks,
create a new revolutionary version control

00:05:54.509 --> 00:05:57.440
system from scratch, and call it Git.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:02.580
Honestly, it’s pretty funny to think that
one of the biggest shortfalls within the software

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:06.760
engineering industry could’ve been solved
or atleast largely improved with just 2 week

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:08.170
of focused work.

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:13.550
But for some reason, no one had decided to
take on this project for decades.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:15.380
Talk about procrastination.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:19.610
Linus would of course turn around and implement
Git into Linux’s repo which immediately

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:23.080
gave Git a strong reputation within the open
source community.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:32.630
But, there was still a long way to go in terms
of making Git mainstream.

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:46.660
GITHUB:
You’re probably wondering, what in the world

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:51.300
did Linus do within just 2 weeks that was
so revolutionary?

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:57.070
Well, he largely reduced the work of code
librarians aka, the gatekeepers.

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:02.430
Before Git, engineers were directly working
on the central codebase and this is why having

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:06.030
highly qualified gatekeepers was so important.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:09.940
These gatekeepers were in charge of making
sure that only high quality code made its

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:14.720
way onto the repo but this also meant that
engineers were spending a lot of their time

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:18.810
just waiting on gatekeepers instead of writing
code.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:23.639
Ironically, this is still the case at most
big tech companies but for different reasons

00:07:23.639 --> 00:07:25.800
like bureaucracy and varying priorities.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:31.780
Back in the day, the issue was mainly due
to a lack of good coding infrastructure.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:34.800
Git, however, largely solved this.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:38.830
Instead of having coders work directly on
the central repository, Git allowed coders

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:41.840
to work on a local version of the repo.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:46.340
This meant that coders could test any change
they wanted locally without the oversight

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:49.220
of gatekeepers or jeopardizing the central
repo.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:53.949
As such, coders were able to better troubleshoot
and validate their code before they ever sent

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:57.710
it to the gatekeepers to verify and merge
with the central repo.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:03.160
This was a revolutionary development but Git
was by no means perfect.

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:06.629
For starters, there was no frontend to Git.

00:08:06.629 --> 00:08:10.150
Everything was handled through the terminal
making it difficult to visualize the history

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:13.050
of changes and modifications to a given repo.

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:17.889
Moreover, sharing code between engineers still
wasn’t all that streamlined or polished,

00:08:17.889 --> 00:08:23.820
and this is where 4 engineers named Tom Preston
Werner, Chris Wanstrath, PJ Hyett, and Scott

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:25.220
Chacon would step in.

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:31.490
In 2008, these 4 would get to work on putting
a face to Git making it easier than ever to

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:34.899
share, fork, modify, push, and merge code.

00:08:34.899 --> 00:08:40.380
For the first time, every developer on a project
could easily view the entire code history,

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:44.790
identify who made what changes, figure out
where things went wrong, and fix conflicts

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.570
and production issues with ease.

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:51.899
If a lot of those terms went over your head,
they basically created an insane code management

00:08:51.899 --> 00:08:57.290
platform based on Git and they called it Logical
Awesome LLC which is surprisingly similar

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:59.149
to the name of this channel.

00:08:59.149 --> 00:09:04.500
Unfortunately though, that name didn’t stick
and they would end up calling it GitHub.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:08.470
Almost immediately, GitHub was a success on
its own.

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:12.790
It didn’t need any venture capital or funding
or loans or any of that.

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:16.860
In fact, the founders were able to manage
all of the expenses of Git by themselves for

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.880
the first 4 years.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:24.740
After all, it’s not that expensive to store
and distribute a bunch of text.

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:29.980
One of the first companies to embrace Git
was none other than Google in 2008.

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:34.560
This was during the peak of their obsession
with opensource software like Android and

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:35.560
Chromium.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:39.640
The knew that if they really wanted to appeal
to the opensource community, they needed to

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:43.350
use the same tools which in this case was
Git.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:47.190
Google would eventually move away from Git
to their own version controlling tool called

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:52.589
Piper but that doesn't really matter because
Google had already put Git and as a result

00:09:52.589 --> 00:09:55.000
Github on the mainstream radar.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:01.380
By 2010, Github was hosting 1 million repos,
and by 2013, they were hosting 10 million

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:02.520
repos.

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:08.300
Most tech companies were just happy to finally
have a modern code management platform but

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:11.200
some wanted a piece of the pie.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:16.560
This of course included VCs who poured hundreds
of millions into GitHub but it also included

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:20.090
Microsoft.

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:32.360
MICROSOFT TAKES CONTROL:
Microsoft didn’t just go straight for the

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:33.360
acquisiton.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.389
Instead, they started off as just a regular
old GitHub user and supporter.

00:10:37.389 --> 00:10:42.820
Starting in 2012, they would move all of their
open source projects, dev tools, and most

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:45.680
of their documentation onto GitHub.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:48.000
This did wonders for GitHub.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:50.290
It’s one thing to support a platform.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:55.300
It’s another to put much of your own code
on that platform, and at this point, both

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:58.490
Google and Microsoft had taken the plunge.

00:10:58.490 --> 00:11:04.060
Let’s just say, it was just a matter of
time until everyone else followed suit.

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:07.870
Microsoft watched all of this playout from
the sidelines for several years before finally

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:09.000
making their move in 2018.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:16.540
The offer was $7.5 billion for the entire
company and frankly this was a deal that the

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:20.670
founders couldn’t turn down as it would
make them billionaires.

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:25.639
But, with that being said, the deal wasn’t
really hostile.

00:11:25.639 --> 00:11:31.000
You see, Microsoft wasn’t trying to takeover
and impose their control over the company.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:35.610
All they really wanted was a star investment
and cloud partner and that’s exactly how

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:36.650
they’ve treated it.

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:40.350
If you don’t believe me, just take it from
GitHub’s CEO himself.

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:45.550
“Microsoft has not forgotten why we did
the deal in the first place and what the important

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:46.850
pillars of the deal are.

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:51.470
The first and foremost principle is to put
developers first.

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:53.889
And that is what we do every day.”

00:11:53.889 --> 00:11:58.730
That’s a far cry from how the founders of
Instagram and WhatsApp feel about Zuckerberg

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:01.320
but that’s a wholenother story.

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:06.670
Anyway, as you can see, things have worked
out great for both GitHub and Microsoft but

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:09.029
what about everyone else?

00:12:09.029 --> 00:12:12.829
Is it healthy and safe for one company to
have so much power?

00:12:12.829 --> 00:12:18.410
Well, if the question is healthy, the answer
is absolutely not.

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:24.250
It is not healthy for one company to control
the codebase of 90% of the fortune 100.

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:30.660
They absolutely need competition and maybe
even regulators but that’ll likely never

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:35.959
happen because GitHub is a background company
that most people don’t even know or care

00:12:35.959 --> 00:12:36.959
about.

00:12:36.959 --> 00:12:41.150
So, as long as they continue lurking in the
background, they shouldn’t have many issues

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:42.570
with regulators.

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:48.300
But while this system is definitely not healthy,
it is safe.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.840
Given GitHub’s business, they’re naturally
a target for hackers.

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:57.380
A few years ago for example, they were subject
to the largest DDoS attack ever recorded.

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:01.920
But we don’t really have to worry about
this because GitHub’s customers know what

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:02.950
they’re doing.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:08.649
Here’s the thing, 99% of the code in the
world doesn’t matter, not in the sense that

00:13:08.649 --> 00:13:13.800
it’s not important but in the sense that
it’s not proprietary.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:19.279
Many of the largest projects in the world
like Chrome and Android are already opensource.

00:13:19.279 --> 00:13:25.040
And even for the projects that aren’t opensource,
the code base doesn’t really matter.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:29.279
Just because you have Netflix or YouTube or
Reddit’s codebase doesn't mean that you’ll

00:13:29.279 --> 00:13:32.580
even have a chance at competing against them.

00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:37.010
For most tech companies, their secret sauce
is their user base, their marketing, their

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:39.829
partnerships, their brand presence, and so
on.

00:13:39.829 --> 00:13:44.220
There’s really nothing proprietary about
making API calls or storing data or handling

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:48.410
errors which is what the vast majority of
the world’s code does.

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:53.180
So, companies aren’t exactly worried about
their codebases getting leaked though they

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:56.089
would of course prefer to not go down that
road.

00:13:56.089 --> 00:14:02.209
There are very few projects and companies
that actually deal with proprietary code where

00:14:02.209 --> 00:14:04.690
the code truly is the hero.

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:08.250
And you can bet that these projects are not
stored on GitHub.

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:12.970
They’re stored on secret databases with
insane levels of security like in the good

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:14.430
old days.

00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:19.450
But aside from these few exceptions, virtually
everything is stored on GitHub and that’s

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:22.640
how Microsoft secretly controls every app
in the entire world.

